exact.text:
The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with initial term $a$ and common difference $2$ is equal to the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric progression with initial term $b$ and common ratio $2$.
\na) Prove that $a+b=2^{a}$;
\nb) Determine $a$ and $b$, if the third term of the geometric progression is equal to the $23$-rd term of the arithmetic progression.

paraphrase.text:
Let the sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence with initial value $m$ and common difference $2$ be the same as the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric sequence with initial value $n$ and common ratio $2$.
\na) Show that $m+n=2^{m}$;
\nb) Determine $m$ and $n$ if the third term of the geometric sequence equals the $23$-rd term of the arithmetic sequence.

style.text:
An arithmetic sequence starts with $a$ and increases by $2$ each term. A geometric sequence starts with $b$ and doubles each term. The sum of the first $b$ terms of the arithmetic sequence equals the sum of the first $a$ terms of the geometric sequence.
\na) Show that $a + b = 2^{a}$;
\nb) Determine $a$ and $b$, if the third term of the geometric sequence is equal to the $23$-rd term of the arithmetic sequence.

exact.text:
Given any sequence of positive integers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{m}$, show that there exists a set $\{b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}\}$, with $n \leq m$, consisting of distinct positive integers such that:
(i) the sum of any two distinct subsets of the $b_i$'s are unequal;
(ii) each $a_i$ can be written as the sum of a subset of $\{b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}\}$.

paraphrase.text:
Given any sequence $x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{m}$ of positive integers, show that there is a set $\{y_{1}, y_{2}, \ldots, y_{n}\}$ of distinct positive integers with $n \leq m$ such that:
(1) the sum of the elements of any subset of $\{y_{1}, y_{2}, \ldots, y_{n}\}$ is unique;
(2) each $x_{i}$ can be expressed as the sum of elements of a subset of $\{y_{1}, y_{2}, \ldots, y_{n}\}$.

exact.text:
In a kingdom, there are $n = 40$ knights. Each day, they pair up for duels in the morning and remain seated around a fixed circular table in the evening. Determine the minimal number of days such that:
\na) all possible pairs of knights have fought at least once;
\nb) every pair of knights has been adjacent at least once during the evenings.

style.text:
There are $40$ warriors in a clan. Each morning, they are paired for sparring (each warrior has exactly one opponent) and each evening, they gather around a fixed seating arrangement at a round table. What is the smallest number of days required such that:
\na) all pairs of warriors have sparred at least once;
\nb) all pairs of warriors have been adjacent at least once around the table?

paraphrase.text:
In acute triangle $ABC$, let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of the altitudes from $B$ and $C$, respectively. Let $P$ be the intersection of line $AM$ with altitude $CC_1$, and $Q$ be the intersection of line $AN$ with altitude $BB_1$. Show that:
\na) $M$, $N$, $P$, and $Q$ lie on a circle;
\nb) if $B$, $C$, $P$, and $Q$ lie on a circle, then $\triangle ABC$ is isosceles.

style.text:
Let $\triangle ABC$ be an acute triangle, and let $B_1$ and $C_1$ be the feet of the altitudes from $B$ and $C$, respectively. Let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of segments $BB_1$ and $CC_1$, respectively. Define $P$ as the intersection of $AM$ with $CC_1$, and $Q$ as the intersection of $AN$ with $BB_1$. Prove that:
\na) The points $M, N, P, Q$ lie on a circle;
\nb) If the points $B, C, P, Q$ lie on a circle, then $\triangle ABC$ is isosceles.

paraphrase.text:
Given the functions $h(x) = 2x^2 + 2x - 4$ and $k(x) = x^2 - x + 2$, determine all real values of $x$ such that:
\na) $\frac{h(x)}{k(x)}$ is a positive integer;
\nb) the inequality $\sqrt{h(x)} + \sqrt{k(x)} \ge \sqrt{2}$ is satisfied.

exact.text:
Let there be $M$ nations and $N$ cities on a planet, with some cities connected by roads. Given:
(1) each nation contains at least three cities;
(2) every city connects by road to at least half of the cities within its nation;
(3) each city has exactly one road connecting it to a city in a different nation;
(4) at most two roads can connect cities in two different nations;
(5) if the total number of cities in two nations is less than $2M$, then there must be at least one road connecting the two nations.
Prove that there is a closed loop involving at least $M + \frac{N}{2}$ cities.

paraphrase.text:
Let $M$ denote the number of countries and $N$ the number of cities on a planet. Some cities are linked by highways. It is given that:
(1) every country contains at least three cities;
(2) each city connects by highways with at least half the cities in its own country;
(3) each city has a single highway connecting it to a city in a different country;
(4) no pair of countries has more than two highways connecting them;
(5) if the total number of cities in two countries is less than $2M$, then there is at least one highway connecting them.
Show that there is a closed path with at least $M + \frac{N}{2}$ cities.

style.text:
Let $C$ be the number of countries and $T$ the number of cities on a world. Some cities are linked by highways. It is given that:
(1) each country has at least three cities;
(2) every city is connected by highways to at least half of the cities in its own country;
(3) each city is connected to exactly one city in a different country;
(4) there are at most two highways connecting cities from two different countries;
(5) if the total number of cities in two countries is less than $2C$, then there is at least one highway between them.
Prove that there exists a cycle that includes at least $C + \frac{T}{2}$ cities.

exact.text:
ext{Let } (a_n), n \in \mathbb{N}, 	ext{ be a sequence of positive real numbers. We say it is *lacunar* if there exists a constant } q > 1 	ext{ such that } a_{n+1} \ge q a_n 	ext{ for all } n. 	ext{ The sequence is called *rare* if there is a positive integer } k 	ext{ such that, for all } x > 0, 	ext{ the open interval } (x, 2x) 	ext{ contains at most } k 	ext{ terms of the sequence.}

ext{a) Does every lacunar sequence satisfy the rare condition?}

ext{b) Is every increasing rare sequence necessarily lacunar?}

\text{Joãozinho escreveu os números de } 1 \text{ até } 10^5 \text{ no quadro. Ele aplicou repetidamente o processo de substituir cada número pela soma de seus dígitos até que todos os números tivessem apenas um dígito. Por exemplo, para } 7234 \text{ obtemos } 7+2+3+4=16 \text{ e depois } 1+6=7.
\na. Que número resultou do número } 98765\text{?}
\nb. Quantas vezes o número } 8 \text{ aparece na lista final?}
\nc. Qual dígito ocorre com maior frequência?

