exact.text:
Gandalf the Wizard devised a magical transformation where each integer is changed into another distinct integer. A number $a$ is said to be *reflecting* if for every integer $x$, the transformed value of $x$ is the same as the transformed value of $a - x$. Can it be true that:
\na. Both $1001$ and $1003$ are reflecting;
\nb. $1000$, $1003$ and $1008$ are all reflecting;
\nc. $1002$, $1004$ and $1006$ are all reflecting?

paraphrase.text:
Suppose a function $ f $ maps each integer to a different integer. An integer $ a $ is said to be *symmetric* if $ f(x) = f(a - x) $ for all integers $ x $. Can the following hold simultaneously:
\na. $ f(1001) = f(1001 - x) $ and $ f(1003) = f(1003 - x) $ for all integers $ x $;
\nb. $ f(1000) = f(1000 - x) $, $ f(1003) = f(1003 - x) $, and $ f(1008) = f(1008 - x) $ for all integers $ x $;
\nc. $ f(1002) = f(1002 - x) $, $ f(1004) = f(1004 - x) $, and $ f(1006) = f(1006 - x) $ for all integers $ x $?

style.text:
Define a number $ a $ as *symmetric* if for all integers $ x $, the transformation $ f $ satisfies $ f(x) = f(a - x) $. Assume that $ f $ is a permutation of the integers with no fixed points. Is it possible that:
\na. $ 2004 $ and $ 2006 $ are both symmetric;
\nb. $ 2001 $, $ 2004 $, and $ 2009 $ are all symmetric;
\nc. $ 2003 $, $ 2005 $, and $ 2007 $ are all symmetric?

