Define sequences $\{a_n\}, \{b_n\}, \{c_n\}$ with $a_1 = 3$, $b_1 = c_1 = 1$ and recurrence relations:
$$
\begin{align*}
 a_{n+1} &= c_n + 3b_n, \\
 b_{n+1} &= a_n + b_n, \\
 c_{n+1} &= c_n + b_n
\end{align*}
$$\nfor $n \ge 2$. Prove that for any odd integer $N \ge 3$,
\ni) there exists an index $m$ such that $a_m - 1$, $b_m$, and $c_m - 1$ are all divisible by $N$;
\nii) for all $n$, at least one of $a_n - 1$, $b_n - 1$, and $c_n - 1$ is not divisible by $N$.

\text{Player A and player B play a game on an } n \times n\text{-board (} n \ge 1\text{). The columns are labeled with powers of 2, from column } 1\text{ to column } 2^{n-1}\text{. Players alternate placing their respective symbols (A places 0, B places 1) on the board, with A going first. Once the board is filled, each row is interpreted as a reversed binary number, where the leftmost digit corresponds to the smallest power of 2. For example, in a 4-column board, the row 0101 represents } 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 2 + 0 \cdot 4 + 1 \cdot 8 = 10\text{.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item For which natural numbers } n \text{ can player B always ensure that at least one of the rows is divisible by 4?}
\item For which natural numbers } n \text{ can player B always ensure that at least one of the rows is divisible by 3?}
\end{enumerate}

